The simplest JavaScript version keeps one pointer called `current`. It reads `data`, pushes that value into an array, and follows `next` until there is no node left.

Dry run with `2 -> 5`: the first loop adds `2`, the pointer moves to the second node, and the next loop adds `5`. The returned array is `[2, 5]`.

```javascript
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode{
    constructor(x, next){
        this.data = x;
        this.next = next;
    }
}
*/

/**
 * @param {ListNode} head
 * @return {Array}
 */

class Solution {
    LLTraversal(head) {
        const values = [];
        let current = head;

        while (current !== null) {
            values.push(current.data);
            current = current.next;
        }

        return values;
    }
}
```

If the list has only one node, the loop runs once. If `head` is null, it returns an empty array without accessing a missing value.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the returned array.

This version uses a small recursive helper. Each call adds the current node's value and then continues with `next`. When the pointer is null, the list is finished.

It is short, but recursion adds stack frames, so a very long list can make an iterative version safer.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/*
Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode{
    public int data;
    public ListNode next;
    ListNode() { data = 0; next = null; }
    ListNode(int x) { data = x; next = null; }
    ListNode(int x, ListNode next) { data = x; this.next = next; }
}
*/

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> LLTraversal(ListNode head) {
        List<Integer> values = new ArrayList<>();
        addValues(head, values);
        return values;
    }

    private void addValues(ListNode node, List<Integer> values) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }

        values.add(node.data);
        addValues(node.next, values);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the result and up to `O(n)` for the recursive call stack.

A direct pointer walk is enough here. Start at `head`, save each `data` value, and move to `Next` until the pointer becomes null.

For example, with `7 -> 3 -> 9`, the result grows as `[7]`, then `[7, 3]`, and finally `[7, 3, 9]`.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

/*
Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode{
  public:
    int data;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode() : data(0), next(nullptr) {}
    ListNode(int x) : data(x), next(nullptr) {}
    ListNode(int x, ListNode *next) : data(x), next(next) {}
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> LLTraversal(ListNode *head) {
        vector<int> values;
        ListNode* current = head;

        while (current != nullptr) {
            values.push_back(current->data);
            current = current->next;
        }

        return values;
    }
};
```

The list itself is not changed. An empty list is handled because the loop is skipped when `head` is null.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, where `n` is the number of nodes.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the returned vector.

A linked list does not support direct indexing, so the pointer must move one node at a time. This Go version counts the nodes first, allocates the answer once, and fills it in order.

The original list stays untouched. The empty-list case returns a non-nil empty slice with length zero.

```go
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
type ListNode struct {
    Data int
    Next *ListNode
}
*/

func LLTraversal(head *ListNode) []int {
    size := 0
    current := head

    for current != nil {
        size++
        current = current.Next
    }

    values := make([]int, size)
    current = head
    index := 0

    for current != nil {
        values[index] = current.Data
        index++
        current = current.Next
    }

    return values
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because the list is visited twice.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the output slice, while the method uses `O(1)` extra space.

